    "body": "consider the following test_script.lua :\r\n\r\n    print 'hello\\n' -- TWO new lines\r\n    print 'this is \\027[31mRED FOR AN AMAZING REASON\\027[0m'\r\n    print 'foo'\r\n    print '' -- newline despite empty!\r\n    print 'bar'\r\n\r\n    -- a bit later:\r\n    os.execute 'sleep 2' -- CPU goes BRR, cursor in column zero\r\n    print 'more bar'\r\n\r\nbefore this PR, `nvim -l test_script.lua` had some weird behaviors compared to `lua test_script.lua` or the now removed `nvim -ll test_script.lua`.\r\n\r\n- one extra '\\n' at the end of a print is ignored, one would need TWO '\\n' for a single extra '\\n'\r\n\r\n- an empty print'' is supposed to print an empty line\r\n\r\n- print'\\027[31mRED' is being escaped so it does not work\r\n\r\n- the cursor gets stuck at the end of a message, instead of after emitting the new line, this looks like the CPU lag has something to do with the _last_ printed message which is likely not the case.\r\n\r\nI consider these behaviors to be unintentional (a consequence of stragglig through the message.c meta-machine, which is optimized for message handling in _an interactive text editor_) and getting closer to plain lua behavior would be better.\r\n\r\nAn alternative would be to restore the builtin print(), but our implemenation still has some benefits, like try\r\n`print(setmetatable({}, {__tostring=error})` in plain lua..\r\n\r\nfix https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/37187\r\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-10-02T11:10:21Z",
